<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis I, Count of Flanders</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1304–1346</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis I, known as Louis of Nevers, was count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, Nevers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rethel">Rethel</a> from 1322 until his death at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-crecy">Battle of Crécy</a> in 1346. Firmly allied with the French crown, he faced the Flemish peasant revolt of 1323–1328 and later the rising of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacob-van-artevelde">Jacob van Artevelde</a>. In 1333 he bought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Liège's rights over Mechelen.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-i-count-of-flanders.jpg" alt="Louis I, Count of Flanders" width="298" height="299" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Olivier De Wree,Lambertus Vossius · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis2Fl.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
